## What the hardware would actually require (silicon vs product-vendor IP)

If you ever rebuilt the stack open-source on this same hardware, the question isn't "ROS 1
vs ROS 2" — it's which drivers you can get. The useful split is **standard silicon (vendor
code or standard interfaces exist)** vs **Airseekers' own application protocols (no substitute)**.

### Standard silicon — drivers/code are available
| Hardware | Silicon | What's available |
|---|---|---|
| NPU | Rockchip **RK3588** | The `librknnrt.so` on this device **is** Rockchip's public SDK (rknpu2, v2.1.0, from Rockchip's GitHub; `rknn-toolkit2` + `rknn_model_zoo`). Freely available, already in use. Not open-source in the full-source sense (runtime is a redistributable blob), but fully published and documented. |
| Stereo camera | **Metoak**, but exposed as **UVC/V4L2** | The camera enumerates as a standard USB UVC device (`/dev/video*`), so **raw stereo frames work without the Metoak SDK** — V4L2 / OpenCV grabs them directly. The SDK (`libMoGeneralSDK`) only adds tuned **depth, rectification and factory calibration**; those are replaceable with OSS stereo (OpenCV SGBM, or a learned stereo network). Note `base_stereo` is `CATKIN_IGNORE`'d here. |
| MCU / mainboard | **Artery AT32** (STM32-class Cortex-M) | Artery publishes HAL/BSP/firmware libraries. The microcontroller itself is standard and documented. |
| GNSS / RTK | **Unicore** | Unicore publishes protocol reference manuals (command/log set); a driver is writable from their docs. NTRIP corrections are already open (`ntrip_client`). |
| Lidar | **Vanjee** | The Vanjee SDK is already present **as source** in this tree (210 headers). Not a gap. |

### Airseekers' own protocols — no vendor or OSS substitute
These sit on top of the standard silicon above and are the vendor's own IP, so no silicon
maker provides them and no OSS equivalent exists. These are the real gaps:
1. **The MCU/mainboard serial protocol** — `mower_base` ↔ the AT32 board over `/dev/ttyUSB0`,
   covering drive motors, blade motor, bumper, charge contacts, buttons and LEDs. The
   application firmware is in `/workspace/mcu_bin/` (`chassis`, `cutter`, `battery`,
   `rtk_rover`, …). The AT32 HAL won't help you talk to *this* board — you'd have to
   reverse-engineer the framing. (OpenMower solves this only for its own hardware.)
2. **The dock/charge integration** (`mower_charge`) — tied to this dock's specific
   detection cues and charge electronics.
3. **The LoRa base-station link** (`base_rtk` / `set_lora`) — the Unicore module and the LoRa
   radio are standard, but the **pairing/framing to Airseekers' base station** is proprietary.

So: the silicon makers do cover their parts (Rockchip, Artery, Unicore, Vanjee), and the
camera is standard UVC — the only genuinely stuck pieces are the three Airseekers-specific
protocols above. Everything above the driver layer (SLAM, perception, localization, planning,
